"""
## Purpose and audience

This specification is maintained for compiler and library engineers reviewing FIXTURES-ACCEPT_RUN behavior, and for operators verifying a build.

## Operator workflow

Run `bin/simple test --no-session-daemon test/fixtures/_accept_run/fail_spec.spl` and confirm the Results line reports 0 failed. Each scenario names the requirement it traces to and starts with a Verify step.

## Compatibility and limitations

Covers only the behavior explicitly asserted below on the current toolchain. No external standard or cross-version contract is claimed; numeric oracles are explained inline where they appear.

## Recovery and troubleshooting

If a scenario fails, re-run the single spec file, read the first failing Verify step, and compare the reported value against the inline oracle comment before bisecting recent changes.
"""
# @manual: primary
# REQ-FIXTURES-ACCEPT_RUN-001
# doc-path: doc/01_research/app/
# doc-path: doc/03_plan/app/
# doc-path: doc/04_architecture/app/
# doc-path: doc/05_design/app/
# =====================================================================
# DELIBERATE FIXTURE — NOT A PRODUCT DEFECT.
# Lane: unstable_test_mode (lane C, test/fixtures/unstable_mode/**).
# This spec FAILS AN ASSERTION ON PURPOSE so the runner's ERROR (real
# failure) outcome class has something honest to detect. Do not "fix" it.
#

# literal selector `tag: "accept"` (test_runner_files.spl, slow path; the manifest
# fast path uses entry.skip_tag). Both markers are therefore required:
# Without the second one the directory run discovers 0 files and reports
# "No test files found" with exit 4 -- measured.
# =====================================================================
use std.spec.*

describe "unstable mode failing-assertion fixture":
    it "fails a genuine assertion on purpose":
        # @req: REQ-FIXTURES-ACCEPT_RUN-001
        step("Verify: fails a genuine assertion on purpose")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        expect(1).to_equal(2)  # oracle: expected value 2 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec
